{:edb/db-before-t 2728 :edb/db-after-t 2729 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536041 50 #inst "2026-10-09T13:31:28.476Z" 13194139536041 true] [17592186184014 1100 "Let $A$ be a $3\\times 5$ matrix. If $\\mathcal{B}= \\{\\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}\\}$ is a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$, find $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))$." 13194139536041 false] [17592186184014 1100 "Let $A$ be a $3\\times 5$ matrix. If $\\mathcal{B}= \\{\\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}\\}$ is a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$, find $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A)).$" 13194139536041 true] [17592186184014 1145 "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=5$, and since $\\mathcal{B}$ is formed by $3$ vectors,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) = 3\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nrank(A) + nullity(A) &= n \\\\\n3 + nullity(A) &= 5 \\\\\nnullity(A) &= 5 - 3 \\\\\ndim(Null(A)) &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536041 false] [17592186184014 1145 "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=5,$ and since $\\mathcal{B}$ is formed by $3$ vectors, $\\textrm{rank}(A) = 3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 3+\\text{nullity}(A) & =5 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =5-3 \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536041 true] [17592186184015 1100 "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, and  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/assets/ba5c9db77780e1298423e58aaf6f9a54968db34643cfe98bfce96231ab6d3afc.png) \n\n- $\\textrm{rank}(A)=1$\n\n- $A$ is invertible\n\n- $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is a $2$-dimensional subspace of $\\Bbb R^3$" 13194139536041 true] [17592186184015 1100 "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, and $\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n1. $\\textrm{rank}(A)=1$\n2. $A$ is invertible\n3. $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is a $2$-dimensional subspace of $\\Bbb R^3$" 13194139536041 false] [17592186184015 1145 "Since $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/assets/db1215d898d9a9af40907393d15bf2a03d1f709d857925e9756cfe15db2f9a07.png)\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have that \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074448/assets/5998f460a1889d2d938c3d530ef86c37f4a76f746ce0885e7892407aa7a2b02f.png) \nwhich means that $\\textrm{nullity}(A) =2.$\n\nNow,  for the matrix $A,$ the number of columns is $n = 3,$ so according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =3 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\textrm{nullity}(A) = 2 \\neq 0,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ cannot be an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\textrm{rank}(A) = 1,$ we have that $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is a $1$-dimensional subspace of $\\Bbb R^3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139536041 true] [17592186184015 1145 "Since $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$$\nCol(A) = R^{3}; dim(Col(A)) = 3 = rank(A); Null(A) = {0}; dim(Null(A)) = 0 = nullity(A)\n$$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhich means that $\\textrm{nullity}(A) =2$.Now, for the matrix $A$, the number of columns is $n = 3$, so according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n$rank(A) + nullity(A)|= n; rank(A) + 2|= 3; rank(A)|= 3 - 2; = 1$.\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\textrm{nullity}(A) = 2 \\neq 0$, then by the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ cannot be an invertible matrix.\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\textrm{rank}(A) = 1$, we have that $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is a $1$-dimensional subspace of $\\Bbb R^3$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139536041 false] [17592186184068 1100 "Consider a $8 \\times 6$ matrix $A$. If $\\textrm{rank}(A)=5$ then what is $\\textrm{nullity}(A)$?" 13194139536041 false] [17592186184068 1100 "Consider a $8 \\times 6$ matrix $A.$ If $\\textrm{rank}(A)=5$ then what is $\\textrm{nullity}(A)?$" 13194139536041 true] [17592186184068 1145 "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=6$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nrank(A) + nullity(A) &= n \\\\\n5 + nullity(A) &= 6 \\\\\nnullity(A) &= 6 - 5 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536041 false] [17592186184068 1145 "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then  \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 5+\\text{nullity}(A) & =6 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =6-5 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536041 true] [17592186184112 1020 #edb/ref 17592186380574 13194139536041 true] [17592186184112 1020 #edb/ref 17592186380588 13194139536041 true] [17592186184112 1020 #edb/ref 17592186380595 13194139536041 true] [17592186184112 1020 #edb/r