gned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139536041 true] [17592186380645 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380646 13194139536041 true] [17592186380645 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380647 13194139536041 true] [17592186380645 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380648 13194139536041 true] [17592186380645 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380649 13194139536041 true] [17592186380645 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380650 13194139536041 true] [17592186380645 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536041 true] [17592186380645 1041 "selection" 13194139536041 true] [17592186380645 1104 #edb/ref 17592186380646 13194139536041 true] [17592186380645 1110 #uuid "7c4cc3b2-fc5b-5ffe-af02-22c8cff4517f" 13194139536041 true] [17592186380646 1034 "II and III only" 13194139536041 true] [17592186380646 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536041 true] [17592186380646 1111 #uuid "8f032b2d-6c34-57d2-b972-b5549065832b" 13194139536041 true] [17592186380647 1034 "I and III only" 13194139536041 true] [17592186380647 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536041 true] [17592186380647 1111 #uuid "97616f3f-c18e-5465-baac-9478b7e86517" 13194139536041 true] [17592186380648 1034 "I only" 13194139536041 true] [17592186380648 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536041 true] [17592186380648 1111 #uuid "9c076e81-b957-5d04-b15a-c58ffdb14cea" 13194139536041 true] [17592186380649 1034 "I and II only" 13194139536041 true] [17592186380649 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536041 true] [17592186380649 1111 #uuid "8b49e5c0-d706-5125-bb5d-a61c435445e9" 13194139536041 true] [17592186380650 1034 "II only" 13194139536041 true] [17592186380650 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536041 true] [17592186380650 1111 #uuid "a185966c-c330-505f-bf04-afe06c599793" 13194139536041 true] [17592186380651 1017 #edb/ref 17592186380652 13194139536041 true] [17592186380651 1089 "q-79423" 13194139536041 true] [17592186380651 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139536041 true] [17592186380651 1100 "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\text{Null}(A)={[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R}?$ \n\n- $\\text{rank}(A)=2$\n- $A$ is **not** invertible\n- $\\text{Col}(A)$ is a $3$-dimensional subspace of ${R}^{4}$" 13194139536041 true] [17592186380651 1113 #uuid "81ca10a0-33db-5c54-bbcd-e87f1a964f72" 13194139536041 true] [17592186380651 1145 "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)={[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R}=\\text{Span}{\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\n \nwe have that $\\text{nullity}(A)=3.$\n\nNow,  for the matrix $A,$ the number of columns is $n=4,$ so according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+3 & =4 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since $\\text{rank}(A)=1\\ne 4,$ it follows, by the invertible matrix theorem, that the matrix $A$ is *not* invertible.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\text{rank}(A)=1$ then $\\text{Col}(A)$ is, in fact,  a $1$-dimensional subspace of ${R}^{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139536041 true] [17592186380652 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380653 13194139536041 t